5. Candidate A — Exact Arbitrary BBOX Cache

Key: workspaceId + minLon + minLat + maxLon + maxLat (as received from Leaflet)

Analysis:

Leaflet calls getBounds() after every moveend and zoomend event. The bounds are IEEE 754 doubles representing the precise geographic extent of the current rendered map canvas. A 1-pixel pan at zoom 14 changes the longitude by approximately 0.0001°. The resulting key is entirely different from the previous one.

- Cache hit rate: Near zero during normal navigation. Hitting the same exact bbox twice requires the user to pan to the exact same pixel position. This almost never happens.
- Key cardinality: Unbounded. Every pan/zoom generates a unique key. A user exploring the map for 5 minutes at high zoom generates hundreds of unique keys, all unreusable.
- Floating-point equality: Double.parseDouble("35.5000001") ≠ Double.parseDouble("35.5000002"). Millisecond timing differences in Leaflet event callbacks can produce slightly different bounds for what the user perceives as the same viewport.
- Memory: Unbounded growth until eviction. With a bounded cache, keys evict before being reused. The eviction is entirely waste.
- Invalidation: Trivial (remove by key on mutation), but pointless since entries are never hit.

Verdict: Not recommended. The arbitrary-bbox approach is maximally cache-unfriendly due to the continuous floating-point viewport. It satisfies the letter of the requirement but provides zero benefit. The task prompt already flagged this concern correctly.

