10. Recommended Cache Target

Workspace-scoped Web Mercator tile cache at a fixed zoom level (z=12).

Cache Areas by the tile that contains their intersecting geometry. Do not cache the entire workspace. Do not cache arbitrary bboxes. PostGIS handles each tile query (on miss) with the full GiST acceleration it already provides.

---
11. Recommended Cache Key

record TileKey(UUID workspaceId, int z, int x, int y) {}

Fixed z=12 for caching. All viewport queries decompose the viewport bbox into z=12 tile coordinates, regardless of the user's current Leaflet zoom level.

Key cardinality: for the prototype workspace and a region the size of Israel (~50 active z=12 tiles in view at any time), cache size stabilizes at 50–200 entries under typical usage.

---
12. Recommended Cached Value

List<AreaResponse>

AreaResponse is an immutable Java record already produced by AreaService.toResponse(). It is fully serializable and detached from the JPA persistence context. Caching DTOs (not JPA entities) avoids persistence context lifecycle issues.

Do NOT cache List<Area> JPA entities — they have lazy-loaded @ManyToOne relationships that become invalid outside the original EntityManager session.

---
13. Read Flow on Cache HIT

GET /workspaces/{workspaceId}/areas?bbox=minLon,minLat,maxLon,maxLat
  ↓
AreaService.findInViewport()
  ↓
parseBbox() → (minLon, minLat, maxLon, maxLat) as doubles
  ↓
workspaceRepository.findById() → workspace exists check
  ↓
bboxToTiles(minLon, minLat, maxLon, maxLat, z=12) → Set<TileKey>
  ↓
for each TileKey:
  cache.getIfPresent(tile) → List<AreaResponse>  (HIT)
  ↓
merge all tile results → deduplicate by Area.id
  ↓
return merged List<AreaResponse>

No PostGIS query. No JDBC. No geometry deserialization.

---
14. Read Flow on Cache MISS

GET /workspaces/{workspaceId}/areas?bbox=minLon,minLat,maxLon,maxLat
  ↓
... same as HIT up to TileKey set ...
  ↓
for each TileKey not in cache:
  tileBbox = tileKeyToBbox(tile)  ← pure math
  areas = areaRepository.findActiveInViewport(workspaceId,
              tileBbox.minLon, tileBbox.minLat,
              tileBbox.maxLon, tileBbox.maxLat)
  responses = areas.stream().map(toResponse).toList()
  cache.put(tile, responses)
  ↓
merge all tile results (cached + freshly loaded) → deduplicate by Area.id
  ↓
return merged List<AreaResponse>

PostGIS handles the spatial query for missed tiles. GiST index is used exactly as before.

Note: Multiple concurrent requests for the same tile could each trigger a DB query (cache stampede). At the prototype's load level (a handful of users), this is not a problem. Caffeine's computeIfAbsent/get(key, mappingFunction) serializes load for the same key within a single JVM.

---
15. CREATE Invalidation Flow

AreaService.createArea() — DB transaction commits
  ↓
@TransactionalEventListener(AFTER_COMMIT)
  ↓
CacheInvalidationListener.onAreaEvent(AreaEventMessage{type=AREA_CREATED, ...})
  ↓
evict all cache entries for workspaceId

New geometry is in AreaEventMessage.payload.geometry (as AreaResponse). For workspace-wide invalidation, the geometry content is not needed — the workspaceId alone drives eviction.

---
16. UPDATE Invalidation Flow

AreaService.updateArea() — DB transaction commits
  ↓
@TransactionalEventListener(AFTER_COMMIT)
  ↓
CacheInvalidationListener.onAreaEvent(AreaEventMessage{type=AREA_UPDATED, ...})
  ↓
evict all cache entries for workspaceId

Critical finding on UPDATE geometry: conditionalUpdate executes a SQL UPDATE (the old geometry is overwritten before the event fires). The old geometry is not captured. The event payload carries only the NEW geometry. Spatially-precise invalidation (evict only tiles covering old AND new geometry) would require an extra SELECT before conditionalUpdate.

For workspace-wide invalidation: this complexity is completely avoided.

For future tile-precise invalidation: add a pre-mutation read of old geometry, compute old tiles, then after commit compute new tiles, union, evict. This is implementable but adds one DB read per UPDATE.

---
17. DELETE Invalidation Flow

AreaService.deleteArea() — DB transaction commits
  ↓
@TransactionalEventListener(AFTER_COMMIT)
  ↓
CacheInvalidationListener.onAreaEvent(AreaEventMessage{type=AREA_DELETED, ...})
  ↓
evict all cache entries for workspaceId

The deleted area's geometry IS in the event payload (AreaResponse). For tile-precise invalidation, we have the geometry. But for the prototype, workspace-wide eviction is used.

---
18. RESTORE Invalidation Flow

AreaService.restoreArea() — DB transaction commits
  ↓
@TransactionalEventListener(AFTER_COMMIT)
  ↓
CacheInvalidationListener.onAreaEvent(AreaEventMessage{type=AREA_RESTORED, ...})
  ↓
evict all cache entries for workspaceId

The restored geometry (from the target AreaVersion) is in the event payload. For workspace-wide eviction: not needed.

