22. Recommended Library/Technology

Caffeine via spring-boot-starter-cache.

Two dependencies to add to pom.xml:
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-cache</artifactId>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>com.github.ben-manes.caffeine</groupId>
    <artifactId>caffeine</artifactId>
</dependency>

Both are managed by Spring Boot 3.5.3's BOM — no <version> needed. Current managed version is Caffeine 3.x.

Spring Cache annotation vs. programmatic access:

@Cacheable is not a good fit here. The findInViewport method decomposes one request into multiple tile keys. @Cacheable supports a single cache key per method invocation. Using it would require either:
- A separate cacheable method per tile (awkward calling convention), or
- Caching the viewport result as a whole (back to Candidate A — arbitrary bbox key)

Programmatic Caffeine access is cleaner:
Cache<TileKey, List<AreaResponse>> tileCache = Caffeine.newBuilder()
    .maximumSize(500)
    .expireAfterWrite(Duration.ofMinutes(5))
    .recordStats()
    .build();

tileCache.get(key, k -> loadFromDB(k)) is Caffeine's atomic load-if-absent method — safe under concurrent access.

---
23. Cache Bounds and TTL

maximumSize(500): 500 tile entries. At z=12, this comfortably covers the entire country of Israel (50–80 tiles), with room for multi-region or larger workspaces. At ~15 KB per tile entry (50 areas × 300 bytes), total memory: 500 × 15 KB = 7.5 MB in the common case; up to 30–40 MB with complex polygons. Defensible.

expireAfterWrite(Duration.ofMinutes(5)): Safety net. Even if mutation-driven invalidation works correctly, stale entries expire after 5 minutes. This bounds the inconsistency window for any invalidation failure.

Eviction policy: Caffeine's W-TinyLFU (Window TinyLFU) — frequency-based eviction that keeps "hot" tiles in cache and evicts cold tiles. Appropriate for a map application where users tend to view the same geographic region repeatedly.

recordStats(): Enables Micrometer metrics (see Section 24).

---
24. Metrics / Observability

Caffeine provides automatic Micrometer integration when:
1. recordStats() is enabled on the cache builder
2. The cache is registered with CaffeineCacheMetrics.monitor(meterRegistry, cache, "viewport.tile")

This automatically exposes:
cache.gets{cache="viewport.tile", result="hit"}     ← counter
cache.gets{cache="viewport.tile", result="miss"}    ← counter
cache.evictions{cache="viewport.tile"}              ← counter
cache.size{cache="viewport.tile"}                   ← gauge

All existing Prometheus scraping at /actuator/prometheus picks these up automatically.

No custom metric code needed. No high-cardinality tags (no workspaceId, no tile coordinates in tags). Cache name tag only.

The hit/miss ratio gives direct visibility into cache effectiveness. If the hit rate is below ~40% during a session, workspace-wide eviction is happening too frequently (writes outpacing reads).

---
25. Expected Performance Benefit

Conditions where cache helps most:
- Multiple users viewing the same geographic region (shared tile cache entries)
- Read-heavy usage (e.g., 50 reads : 1 write ratio)
- Remote production database (each cache hit saves a ~10–50 ms round-trip)
- Large result sets (many polygons per tile — geometry deserialization is skipped)

Quantified estimate (production scenario):
- PostGIS tile query: ~20 ms (remote DB, 50 areas returned)
- Cache hit: ~0.1 ms (in-process Caffeine lookup)
- Speedup per hit: ~200×
- At 70% hit rate and 5 users × 2 reads/sec = 10 reads/sec: 7 reads/sec served from cache → save 7 × 20 ms = 140 ms/sec of DB wait → meaningful for a shared connection pool

Local development (prototype):
- PostGIS tile query: ~5 ms (same Docker host)
- Cache hit: ~0.1 ms
- Speedup per hit: ~50×
- Measurable, but PostGIS is already fast locally

---
26. Cases Where the Cache Will Not Help

1. Active collaborative editing (many mutations): Workspace-wide eviction on each mutation resets all tile entries. If 5 users are drawing at 10 actions/min each = 50 writes/min = ~1 write/1.2 sec, the cache is continually invalidated. The effective cache lifetime is ~1.2 seconds. In a viewport query every 2 seconds (on pan), the hit rate drops below 50%.
2. Single user, single session: Only one user panning around means no cache sharing. Some tiles are reused within a session (panning back), but the benefit is limited.
3. Local development with Docker on same host: PostGIS is already very fast (~5 ms). The cache overhead (Caffeine lookup + merge + deduplication) may be ~1–2 ms. Net benefit is small.
4. Complex polygon geometries: If each area has hundreds of vertices, JDBC deserialization is significant. Cache saves this cost on hits. But it also means each cache entry is larger (more memory pressure, more evictions).
5. High-write rate approaching rate limit maximum: At 50 mutations/min, workspace-wide eviction occurs every ~1.2 sec. The cache benefits only queries that happen to run between mutations.
6. Very large workspaces: If a z=12 tile contains 5,000 areas (all clustered in a small geographic region), each cache entry is ~2.5 MB. 500-entry cache limit means only ~200 MB maximum but maximumSize is in entries not bytes, so a few dense-tile entries can consume all memory. Mitigation: add maximumWeight by byte size rather than entry count, or reduce maximumSize.

